@page
@model HealthcareSupport.CaseEvaluation.Pages.Account.LockedOutModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Account locked";
}
@*
    Custom Razor view for /Account/LockedOut. Filesystem precedence
    overrides the stock AbpAccountPublicWeb RCL page so this view + its
    PageModel substitute for the stock pair. Layout mirrors the existing
    ResetPassword / ForgotPassword overrides.

    Behaviour: shows a plain-language "account locked" message picking
    the failed-attempts cause (the only path that reaches this redirect
    today; admin-locks would route differently if ever surfaced).

    Item D (2026-08-22): the duration is NO LONGER hard-coded. Lockout is
    progressive (1 -> 5 -> 15 minutes -> the configured maximum), so a
    fixed "1 hour" was wrong for most lockouts and overstated the wait by
    up to 59 minutes -- which sends people to the phone over something
    that has already expired.

    This page still cannot look the user up: the framework redirect
    carries no userId. LoginModel computes the real remainder, where the
    submitted identifier is still in hand, and passes it via TempData;
    absent that, Model.RemainingText degrades to "a short while" rather
    than inventing a number.

    The sentence supplies the "in"; RemainingText is always a bare noun
    phrase. This line first read "Try again @Model.RemainingText." --
    correct for the fallback, but it rendered "Try again about 5 minutes"
    as soon as a real ladder duration arrived (found on the box,
    2026-08-26). Keep the preposition here and the phrase there.

    "You can sign in again in X" rather than "Try again in X" because the
    page has to answer one question: is waiting SUFFICIENT, or is some
    other step required? It previously carried a password-reset link
    alongside the wait, which left both readings open (Adrian, 2026-08-26)
    -- so the link is gone and the sentence states plainly that the wait
    is all there is. Anyone who is locked out BECAUSE they have forgotten
    their password reaches "Forgot password?" from the sign-in page, one
    click away via the link below.

    Do not reintroduce a reset link here without resolving that ambiguity
    in the wording -- completing a reset does still clear the lockout
    (ExternalAccountAppService.ResetPasswordAsync zeroes the failed count
    and nulls LockoutEnd), so the temptation to advertise it will recur.
*@

<div class="account-card">
    <div class="account-content">

        <h1 class="text-center mt-4 mb-3">Account locked</h1>

        <p class="text-center mt-3" role="alert">
            Too many failed sign-in attempts. You can sign in again in @Model.RemainingText.
        </p>

        <p class="text-center mt-2">
            If this wasn't you, contact the clinic.
        </p>

        <hr class="my-4" />

        <p class="text-center">
            <a href="~/Account/Login">&larr; Back to sign-in</a>
        </p>

    </div>
</div>
